DTEND:20261002T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Kajari Saha\, University of California\, Santa B
 arbara - Weather Shocks\, Credit Access and Intimate Partner Violence in
  India
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Kajari Saha\, University of California\, Santa Barba
 ra\nTitle: "Weather Shocks\, Credit Access and Intimate Partner Violence
  in India"\n\nThis study examines how weather shocks affect women’s ex
 posure to intimate partner violence (IPV) in rural India. I link geocode
 d weather data to women’s reports of IPV from the 2015–16 and 2019
 –21 rounds of India’s National Family Health Survey. Exposure to dro
 ught and extreme heat during the primary growing season significantly in
 creases IPV. Growing-season drought raises overall IPV by 11.3\\% and ph
 ysical IPV by 11.8\\%\, while heat has smaller but significant effects. 
 Consistent with an income channel\, access to formal finance mitigates t
 he effect of drought on IPV. At the household level\, access to bank acc
 ounts attenuates the drought–IPV relationship. At the district level\,
  the composition of credit matters: agricultural lending - particularly 
 credit provided directly to farmers - significantly attenuates the effec
 t of drought on IPV\, while indirect\, agribusiness-linked agricultural 
 credit and non-agricultural credit do not. Finally\, the substantially l
 arger effects of wet-bulb heat extremes relative to dry-bulb heat extrem
 es are consistent with a more immediate\, heat-induced physiological str
 ess channel. JEL Classification Codes: J12\, J16\, O13\, O16\, Q56\n\nht
 tps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/job-market-practice-talk-kajari-saha
 -university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/job-market-practice-talk-kajari
 -saha-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:66c02bbfa6c741fc8cbb@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260929T182836Z
DTSTART:20261002T183000Z
DTEND:20261002T200000Z
SUMMARY:Other Events: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara - Screening and Compliance Documentation as Complements? Ef
 fects on Fraud and Administrative Burden
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara\nTitle: "Screening and Compliance Documentation as Complemen
 ts? Effects on Fraud and Administrative Burden"\n\nThis paper investigat
 es whether compliance documentation offer incremental fraud deterrence b
 eyond initial ex-ante screening in public programs by leveraging a polic
 y feature of the Paycheck Protection Program - a loan-size threshold tha
 t waived compliance documentation requirements for loan forgiveness. On 
 one hand\, the waiver may encourage firms with true loan eligibility bel
 ow the threshold to opportunistically inflate their loan requests up to 
 the threshold. On the other hand\, firms with true loan amounts above th
 e threshold may strategically reduce their loans to avoid the administra
 tive burden of compliance. Quantitatively\, opportunistic overclaiming w
 as limited\, and the loan-reducing effect outweighed the loan-increasing
  effect\, resulting in a net welfare gain of $0.03 per dollar borrowed f
 rom the documentation waiver. More broadly\, when screening is in place\
 , a higher probability of random audit can substitute for universal comp
 liance documentation to limit fraud. Documentation requirements may impo
 se substantial compliance burdens while providing limited marginal fraud
  deterrence beyond the initial screening process. JEL Classification Cod
 es: D04\, D21\, D22\, D60\, H81\, H83\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/al
 l/2026/amel-donovan-rasamoelison-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/amel-donovan-rasamoelison-unive
 rsity-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:32eb24e68c6395c116cc@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260929T182836Z
DTSTART:20261007T223000Z
DTEND:20261008T000000Z
SUMMARY:Other Events: Yihong Liu\, University of California\, Santa Barba
 ra - Matched Triple Differences: A Framework for Covariate Adjustment
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Yihong Liu\, University of California\, Santa Barbar
 a\nTitle: "Matched Triple Differences: A Framework for Covariate Adjustm
 ent"\nHosted by: Daniel Martin\n\nIdentification in triple-differences (
 DDD) designs is often justified by a conditional parallel gaps assumptio
 n\, under which units with similar observed covariates are expected to e
 xhibit similar deviations from parallel trends. In this setting\, the co
 mmon empirical estimation strategy takes one of the two forms: adding th
 e covariates\, or their interactions with time indicators\, to a three-w
 ay fixed effects (3WFE) regression. We formally study both practices and
  show that\, in general\, neither consistently estimates the average tre
 atment effect on the treated (ATT). Our diagnosis has two parts. First\,
  the OLS estimands of both 3WFE specifications fail to satisfy the covar
 iate balancing condition that is sufficient for them to equal the ATT. S
 econd\, we characterize the additional restrictive assumptions for each 
 OLS estimand to equal the ATT. To address this limitation\, we propose a
  \\emph{matched triple-differences} framework that achieves the sufficie
 nt covariate balance for consistent estimation. Within this framework\, 
 we construct a class of consistent matching estimators\, establish their
  asymptotic normality\, and provide a consistent variance estimator acco
 unting for the variability introduced by matching. The framework accommo
 dates a general class of matching procedures\, including nearest-neighbo
 r matching and kernel matching. Our empirical application to \\cite{Cai_
 2016_AEJ} shows that our proposed matching estimators can yield estimate
 s and qualitative conclusions that differ from those obtained from the 3
 WFE regression. Keywords: Triple-Differences\; Difference-in-Differences
 -Differences\; Parallel Gaps\; Matching JEL Classification: C14\, C21\, 
 C23\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-yihong-li
 u-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-yihong-liu-un
 iversity-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cfe882721bc981f0d7ba@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260929T182836Z
DTSTART:20261008T223000Z
DTEND:20261009T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Matias Strehl-Pessina\, University of California
 \, Santa Barbara - Can Targeted VAT Exemptions Make VAT Less Regressive?
  Evidence from a Welfare-Program Reform in Uruguay
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Matias Strehl-Pessina\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara\nTitle: "Can Targeted VAT Exemptions Make VAT Less Regressi
 ve? Evidence from a Welfare-Program Reform in Uruguay"\n\nVAT taxes are 
 a major source of public revenue\, especially in Latin America\, but the
 y are often regressive. Traditional VAT exemptions for basic goods are c
 ostly\, poorly targeted\, and may be partly captured by firms through hi
 gher retail prices. This paper studies an alternative policy design: a t
 argeted VAT rebate for low-income households beneficiaries of a welfare 
 program. I ask whether participating retailers responded by increasing p
 osted prices\, thereby capturing part of the intended benefit. Combining
  administrative program records with scanner retail data\, I use a diffe
 rence-in-differences design comparing stores with different pre-reform e
 xposure to program-card sales. I find that posted prices increased by ab
 out 3 to 4 percent only in stores highly dependent on program sales\, wh
 ich represent a small share of total program purchases. For most program
  purchases\, prices did not increase. The results suggest that targeted 
 VAT exemptions can reduce VAT regressivity while avoiding fiscal spendin
 g on higher-income households and minimizing retailer capture\, though m
 arket conditions remain important for incidence. JEL Clasification Codes
 : H21\, H22\, H23\, I38\, L81\, O12\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/
 2026/practice-job-talk-matias-strehl-pessina-university-california-santa
 -barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-matias-strehl
 -pessina-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:68d3b6cf75c14b6f3a42@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260929T182836Z
DTSTART:20261013T223000Z
DTEND:20261014T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Camila Paleo\, University of California\, Santa 
 Barbara - Temporary Jobs\, Lasting Credit? Evidence from Uruguay's Publi
 c Works Program
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Camila Paleo\, University of California\, Santa Barb
 ara\nTitle: "Temporary Jobs\, Lasting Credit? Evidence from Uruguay's Pu
 blic Works Program"\n\nPublic works programs are common in developing co
 untries\, but evidence on their long-run effects remains limited. We est
 imate the effects of Uruguay's Oportunidad Laboral\, a temporary public 
 employment program\, on participants' financial well-being. Leveraging l
 ottery-based assignment and linked administrative records from the Centr
 al Bank of Uruguay\, we show that participation sharply increases formal
  employment and earnings during the program\, with no persistent formal-
 sector labor market effects after exit. In contrast\, formal credit use 
 rises persistently: by 8 percentage points during participation and by 8
 .4 percentage points in the 15-month post-program period\, consistent wi
 th temporary formal employment relaxing barriers to financial inclusion.
  However\, credit expansion also increases repayment risk. Delinquency r
 ises by 2.4 percentage points during the program and by 10.7 percentage 
 points afterward\, with effects persisting 31 months after exit. Effects
  are larger for transgender applicants\, smaller in high-unemployment mu
 nicipalities\, and delinquency effects are concentrated among new borrow
 ers. The results show that public works programs can affect welfare thro
 ugh financial markets\, but credit expansion without sustained earnings 
 may increase financial vulnerability. JEL Classification Codes: G51\, I3
 8\, J68\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-camil
 a-paleo-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-camila-paleo-
 university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f3bb9256a6594c739760@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260929T182836Z
DTSTART:20261014T223000Z
DTEND:20261015T000000Z
SUMMARY:Other Events: Juan Nicolas Herrera\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara - Value-Added Tax Incidence in Housing Markets
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Juan Nicolas Herrera\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara\nTitle: "Value-Added Tax Incidence in Housing Markets"\nHost
 ed by: Daniel Martin\n\nWe study the VAT incidence in the housing market
 \, exploiting a 2016 reform inChile that introduced a 19% tax on sales b
 y developers while exempting resales by in-dividual homeowners. Using ri
 ch administrative data covering nearly the universe ofresidential transa
 ctions\, we estimate a difference-in-differences design that leveragesth
 e differential tax treatment across seller types. Apartment prices per s
 quare meterrose by approximately 11.8% within three years\, with consume
 rs bearing 75% of theeffective tax rate. This increase combines two marg
 ins: an 8.5% rise in nominal pricesand a 3.2% decline in unit sizes\, a 
 form ofhousing shrinkflationvisible in constructionpermits before any pr
 ice response. Despite the large tax\, transaction volumes did notdecline
 . Purchase contracts surged by 33% before implementation and fell by a c
 om-parable amount immediately after\, indicating pure re-timing rather t
 han a permanentdemand response. The tax raised revenue without reducing 
 market activity\, but at thecost of higher prices and smaller apartments
  for buyers. JEL Classification Codes: H22\, H25\, R21\, R31\n\nhttps://
 econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-juan-nicolas-herrera-uni
 versity-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-juan-nicolas-
 herrera-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b3bbecaa8de4c9c6ce31@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260929T182836Z
DTSTART:20261015T223000Z
DTEND:20261016T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Reetika Sindhi\, University of California\, Sant
 a Barbara - The Long Shadow of Communal Violence: Childhood Exposure and
  Later-Life Mental Health
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Reetika Sindhi\, University of California\, Santa Ba
 rbara\nTitle: "The Long Shadow of Communal Violence: Childhood Exposure 
 and Later-Life Mental Health"\n\nWe study the long-run relationship betw
 een childhood exposure to communal violence and women’s mental health 
 in India. Using a newly constructed crosswalk spanning seven decades\, w
 e link historical Hindu--Muslim riot locations to respondents’ childho
 od districts in the Longitudinal Ageing Study in India (LASI). Exploitin
 g variation in exposure across childhood districts and birth cohorts\, w
 e find that exposure to at least one riot between birth and age~14 is as
 sociated with a 2.4 percentage point higher probability of major depress
 ive disorder in later life\, or 29 percent of the sample mean. Greater c
 umulative exposure is similarly associated with worse later-life mental 
 health. The association is strongest for exposure at ages 0--4 and 10--1
 4\, consistent with differences in vulnerability across developmental st
 ages. Childhood riot exposure is also associated with a higher likelihoo
 d of child marriage\, pointing to changes in life-course trajectories as
  a potential channel. These findings show that even localized\, short-li
 ved episodes of communal violence can have mental-health consequences th
 at persist for decades. JEL Classification Code: D74\, I12\, I31\, J12\,
  J14\, J16\, N35\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-t
 alk-reetika-sindhi-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-reetika-sindh
 i-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
